statement:
A machine has **64-byte cache lines**. You walk a large array of 4-byte `int`s from start to finish, one element at a time:

```c
long sum = 0;
for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
    sum += a[i];
```

The array is far too big to fit in any level of cache, so nothing is still resident from a previous pass, and assume no hardware prefetching.

**What fraction of the `n` accesses miss in the cache?**

Give it as a fraction, a decimal or a percentage — `1/16`, `0.0625` and `6.25%` are all read as the same number.
